{"step_index":2584,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-27T11:33:34Z","input_tokens":2660,"cache_read_tokens":78160,"output_tokens":4931,"thinking":"Observed that `renderRoadmapOverviewCard` utilizes `p.phase`. Considering that both `stage` and `phase` might be used interchangeably, a unified approach suggests defining stage objects with both keys. This ensures consistent data access regardless of the chosen key, improving flexibility. The proposed object structure includes both `stage` and `phase` as aliases, alongside other descriptive properties.\n\n\nThe first stage description emphasizes fundamental Python list operations: in-place swaps, traversals, frequency counting, and prefix sums. This suggests that the initial learning phase centers on array manipulation techniques. The milestone, Two Sum, likely leverages these foundational concepts.\n\n\nStage three focuses on optimizing quadratic time complexity algorithms to linear time via two-pointer techniques and sliding windows. This includes handling scenarios with converging pointers and dynamically adjusting window sizes, potentially employing hash tables to maintain window state. Stage four introduces prefix sums, sorting, and binary search, offering O(1) range queries and logarithmic time search through sorted data. This may involve binary search on the answer space.\n\n\nStage five introduces linked lists, stacks, and queues, focusing on heap-allocated node manipulation and state buffering with LIFO and FIFO structures.  Key concepts include handling edge cases with sentinel nodes and employing fast/slow pointer techniques.  These concepts lead to problems like parentheses validation and linked list reversal.\n\nStage six shifts to recursion and backtracking, emphasizing recurrence relations and state-space exploration through decision trees.  This phase covers permutation, combination, and power set problems, implementing the \"choose, explore, unchoose\" pattern with pruning to avoid exponential complexity.\n\nStage seven initiates the study of trees and binary search trees.\n\n\nStage seven introduces trees and binary search trees, focusing on traversal algorithms (preorder, inorder, postorder, level-order) and the BST invariant. Prefix tries are also introduced for efficient string lookups. The problems cover maximum depth and BST validation.\n\nStage eight shifts to heaps and graphs, covering priority queues, graph representations (adjacency lists), shortest path finding with BFS, cycle detection with DFS, topological sorting, and Union-Find algorithms. The milestones involve the \"Number of Islands\" and \"Course Schedule\" problems.\n\n\nStage nine introduces greedy algorithms and dynamic programming. Key concepts include identifying optimal substructure and overlapping subproblems, top-down memoization, bottom-up tabulation, and greedy interval merging. Stage ten covers advanced data structures, bit manipulation, and math concepts. This includes in-place matrix transformations, modular arithmetic, and composite structures.\n\n\nConsidering `getStageStats`, it appears designed to provide a comprehensive summary of a given stage's progress. The function gathers problem data, calculates completion percentages, tracks attempted problems, and determines the status of the stage's milestone. It also seems to identify the next unsolved problem and track the difficulty distribution.\n\n\nAnalyzing the completion rules, `status` determination prioritizes meaningful progress. The function increments difficulty counters. A stage is `mastered` only with 100% completion or significant problem-solving (minimum of 5 solved and 50% completion) alongside milestone completion. Otherwise, the status is `in_progress` if any problems were attempted or solved. The stage remains `not_started` if no problems have been attempted or solved.\n\n\nThe system allows flexible stage navigation; any stage can be set as active, regardless of prior stage completion. The `getActiveStage` and `setActiveStage` methods manage the active stage, storing the selection locally and visually highlighting the active stage. The `practiceNextInStage` method opens the next unsolved problem in a selected stage, utilizing stage statistics.\n\nThe `exploreStageProblems` method enables stage-specific problem exploration, offering a filterable problem table by stage. This stage filtering feature refines problem selection. By setting `selectedStage`, the problems table focuses on a specific stage's problems.\n\n\nThe full roadmap view presents a header with overall KPIs and a subtitle. It includes a navigation bar for quick stage jumps, a filter bar for stage status, and a spotlight card for the active stage. The timeline displays individual stage cards, each with a header, active stage indicator, and status badge.\n\n\nThe roadmap overview card displays stage-specific learning elements, including completion stats, core concepts, and curated problems. Stage filtering relies on a `filterProblems()` function that filters the problem set based on the `selectedStage`. The filtering logic accesses `BEGINNER_ROADMAP_10_STAGES` and uses the stage's topics to determine problem inclusion. Problems are included if they have any of the stage's topics, or the stage's topics match the problem's `topic` or `primaryTopic` properties.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/app_build/dsa_dashboard.js","EndLine":1420,"StartLine":1350,"toolAction":"Viewing sub-views rendering in dsa_dashboard.js","toolSummary":"View sub-views rendering in dsa_dashboard.js"}}]}
